from pathlib import Path
import re,json
HERE=Path(__file__).resolve().parent;ROOT=HERE.parents[3];ONT=ROOT/'.g5/ontology'
changes=[]
def amend(file,id,addition,replace=None):
 p=ONT/file;s=p.read_text();pat=r'(^\[[IDQ] · '+re.escape(id)+r'\]\n)(.*?)(?=\n<a id=|\n## |\Z)';m=re.search(pat,s,re.S|re.M);assert m,(file,id)
 old=m[2];new=(replace if replace is not None else old.rstrip()+'\n\n'+addition.strip()+'\n')
 if addition.strip() and addition.strip() in old:return
 s=s[:m.start(2)]+new+s[m.end(2):];p.write_text(s);changes.append({'file':file,'id':id})
def child(file,id,parent,kind,text,evidence):
 p=ONT/file;s=p.read_text()
 if '['+kind+' · '+id+']' in s:return
 marker='## Metadata';assert marker in s
 s=s.replace(marker,f'<a id="{id}"></a>\n[{kind} · {id}]\n\n{text}\n\n'+marker,1)
 s+=f'\n### {id}\n\n- agent-provided\n- user-facing-behavior\n- Expands: [{parent}](#{parent}).\n\nEvidence: {evidence}\n'
 p.write_text(s);changes.append({'file':file,'id':id})
ev='../../uplift/consolidated/extension/REPORT.md'
amend('features/load.g5.md','load.outcome','''- This rollback rule applies to database writes made before the final commit, with journaling enabled. A failed after-install message or cleanup after that commit returns an error with the module kept ([late errors](#load.status.after-commit)).
- Child flags are resolved separately on each activation. An absent parent key permits the child's own default; a defined parent value, including zero, wins. Child `NoTransaction` skips its own `TSTART` and leaves an existing parent transaction open. Child `NoJournal` can make its writes survive parent rollback. Their intended policy stays in the linked child questions.
- Class descriptors and usable code are separate outcomes. After two-resource compile failure, ordinary rollback removes both classes. Without transactions, or after the developer commit, both descriptors may remain while only the first class runs. This relies on the observed IRIS compiler behavior, not on a proof of the compiler.''')
amend('features/load.g5.md','load.status','''As built, an error can also follow the final commit. The caller must read the stored module and committed history result separately from status ([late errors](#load.status.after-commit)); a successful message and a brace-free macro are distinct valid neighbors.''')
amend('features/load.g5.md','load.dependencies.local-repo','''- Cache creation failure can leave no row but retain its allocated id in the manager. Cleanup may warn about that missing row (C:D2). At the top-level cleanup call the module can already be committed; at a dependency call the parent rolls back, retaining its first manifest commit only in developer mode.
- A higher candidate version wins before repository order. Equal versions choose the older repository id in the native observations; temporary sort position does not force local priority (C:Q1).''')
amend('features/history.g5.md','history.survives-rollback','''As built, a dependency's permanent record inserted inside its parent's transaction can disappear with the parent's rollback (G:D1 = P:F-HIST-1). Its successful result in IRISTEMP, installed data, and permanent row are three separate observations. This contradicts the survival requirement; it does not redefine success.''')
amend('features/history.g5.md','history.dependencies.parent-rollback','''- Settled meaning: `Success` is the child's own result, even if a later parent rollback undoes its changes; `Committed` describes kept database writes. The requirement for a distinct surviving row is source-backed by the history design discussion (P DEC-HIST-1; property and PR578 review). As built the row can be absent. The caller-transaction policy remains a separate question.''')
amend('features/history.g5.md','history.committed.nested','''- Settled observation: the command may return success before the caller later rolls back its module data and permanent history row. The imported model proves this no-trace result under its stated domain. Published caller-transaction use does not decide the independent audit retention policy; the working assumption and alternatives above remain policy proposals.''')
amend('features/history.g5.md','history.committed','''`Committed` describes database writes; it does not promise to undo copied files, lock files, pip effects or CPF changes. The property comment and maintainer history design review settle this field meaning (P DEC-HIST-5). Inherited [side-effects question](#history.committed.side-effects) is retained as an alias for that resolved interpretation. After a post-commit error the record can be committed and unsuccessful.''')
amend('features/history.g5.md','history.committed.side-effects','',replace='''\nThis question is resolved for the field's meaning: `Committed` covers database changes. It makes no filesystem rollback promise. The property comment and maintainer explanation recorded in P DEC-HIST-5 support this reading; the wiki's “written to disk” wording is broader and needs correction. Filesystem effects remain observable even when this bit is false.\n''')
amend('features/history.g5.md','history.read.filter','''As built, `ConstructSQLWhere` walks keys in IRIS collation order and stops at the first absent value or name outside `[A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9]*`. It keeps earlier predicates and ignores that key and every later one. An underscore column such as `Version_Major` can therefore leave no predicate: an unmatched `Version_Major=99` deleted all three seeded identities. A valid `Package=del-b` or `Success=0` deleted only its matches. An unknown alphanumeric column raises SQL error; `find` can hide that error behind an empty successful listing while delete throws. These are failures of the rule, not permitted filtering behavior (G:D2).''')
amend('features/history.g5.md','history.delete','''The intended exact-selection rule above is violated by the filter parser's stop rule ([filter conditions](#history.read.filter)). Neither the presence of a syntactic filter nor `-confirm` ensures an effective predicate. Global delete ignores namespace exceptions in the as-built loop. A valid filter's exact deleted identities remain part of the guarantee.''')
amend('features/history.g5.md','history.read.api','''The two global readers differ as built. CLI `find -globally` fetches each result in its own namespace, labels that namespace and applies the limit per namespace. `GlobalRecordsFetch` fetches each handle in the caller's namespace, so it repeats the caller's rows; its row schema includes neither namespace nor ID. Correct CLI output does not establish programmatic query correctness (G:D20).''')
amend('features/history.g5.md','history.retention','''As built, the quoted cutoff can keep an expired row while `SetHistoryLastPurge` advances the daily marker (P:F-HIST-6; native P1–P4). The marker proves an attempt, not successful deletion. Preserve record identities when checking retention.''')
amend('features/history.g5.md','history.delete.running-records','''- Source/observation boundary: an in-memory twin and the database row can differ. A new held-out stale-writer interleaving deleted the twin, then finalized and persisted; persist returned OK and the row stayed absent. This narrows the prior resurrection hypothesis. It does not establish every concurrent lock schedule. A dependency row may be protected by the parent's transaction lock; top-level rows do not share that premise. Keep true concurrent and cross-namespace schedules open.''')
amend('concepts/transactions-and-dev-mode.g5.md','transaction.rollback-scope','',replace='''\nA database rollback undoes journaled writes in its transaction. It does not undo filesystem copies, lock files, CPF effects, pip installs or remote calls. The database-only interpretation of the history bit is source-backed (P DEC-HIST-5). New native FileCopy tests kept a copied file after a failing load rolled its module record back; a deferred-copy neighbor left no file.\n\nThe broader policy question is [external effects](#transaction.rollback-scope.policy); CPF's real merge/rollback behavior remains an explicit environment hypothesis until exercised safely.\n''')
child('concepts/transactions-and-dev-mode.g5.md','transaction.rollback-scope.policy','transaction.rollback-scope','Q','''Should a failed package change compensate external effects that the database cannot roll back?

- Evidence: copied files and `module-lock.json` survived native database rollback; arbitrary hooks and remote calls can have further effects.
- Working assumption: database rollback is the existing boundary; external effects need separate receipts and must be reported.
- Alternative: add explicit compensating actions for each external writer.
- Affected claims: load failure atomicity, lock-file location and status, file copy, CPF and history `Committed`.''','Native held-out FileCopy/load receipts in '+ev+'. No product repair applied.')
amend('concepts/transactions-and-dev-mode.g5.md','transaction.flag-sources','''The effective values are merged by key at each `LoadNewModule` activation. A parent parameter that exists, even zero, overrides the child's default. If it is absent, the child's own value applies. Child `NoTransaction` does not cancel an already-open parent transaction; child `NoJournal` disables process journaling during that activation and can escape rollback. These are settled source/native facts; the linked questions ask whether to preserve that policy.''')
amend('concepts/modules.g5.md','module.env','''Object elements in arrays merge recursively at the same array index: `[{"x":1}]` followed by `[{"y":2}]` keeps both keys. Primitive arrays merge by position: `[1,2]` then `[3]` yields `[3,2]`. Both direct merge and the actual file loader have receipts. A missing or malformed later file clears earlier values and raises; each `LoadEnvironmentConfig` call clears first. Process-local config reaches a same-process compile, while a fresh worker process starts empty. These are observed implementation rules; C:Q6 below keeps the desired failure/merge policy open.''')
amend('concepts/modules.g5.md','module.parameter.expressions','''As built, a value without `{` returns unchanged before parameter lookup, macro expansion or expression compilation. With `{`, lookup and nested placeholders run, followed by macro/expression evaluation. A replacement containing `$` may raise; a dotted parameter name can match another name because it becomes regex syntax. A cyclic reference exhausts the loop before the declared cycle error can run (C:D3–D5). These failures contradict literal-name/value intent. C:Q2 asks whether brace-free macros should expand; it does not leave the observed gate undecided.''')
amend('concepts/modules.g5.md','module.processor.file-copy','''Immediate copies run before Activate; `Defer` runs them after successful Activate. A failing hook between them leaves an immediate target but prevents the deferred copy. Database rollback does not restore either filesystem state. With `KeepOnUninstall`, Clean preserves the target; otherwise Clean removes it. Directory overlay rules protect system directories. These cases were checked through both the processor and an actual failing load; no general filesystem compensation is proved.''')
amend('concepts/repositories.g5.md','repo.oras','''The ORAS availability adapter accepts a successful HTTP 2xx reply or a 401 reply carrying `WWW-Authenticate`. A headerless 401 and a 403 are unavailable. This is an availability probe, not proof that authentication, pulling or publishing will work. Native loopback HTTP cases distinguish all four replies.''')
amend('features/dependencies.g5.md','deps.lockfile','''As built, lock-file writing happens before later lifecycle phases. A later failed load can roll its module record back while the lock file remains. A write error is logged and can be swallowed rather than failing the load. When the new stored module still has an empty Root, native `load -lock` wrote relative `module-lock.json` in the process working directory instead of the requested module root. A direct helper with a populated Root is a distinct neighbor. The intended root-location rule above is preserved; this observation is a defect, not an exception authorizing another location.''')
amend('features/build-and-publish.g5.md','build.test-output','''The output-file export happens before summary rendering. An unsupported summary format can therefore return an error after writing valid JSON results; the same passing tests with a supported summary format return success. Command status and report-file existence are separate observations. Verification uses a different namespace lifecycle, so a test reporting check does not prove verify cleanup.''')
# The B7 precision case belongs with the existing sync rule.
p=ONT/'features/developer-tools.g5.md';s=p.read_text();ids=re.findall(r'^\[[IDQ] · ([^\]]*sync[^\]]*)\]',s,re.M)
if ids:amend('features/developer-tools.g5.md',ids[0],'''A failed sync with an existing baseline retains SyncFailed and retries even after exact-byte revert. Outside-sync compile/load/reload can change server code without a retry mark, so an exact revert can then hide that change (OUT premise/P:F-SYNC-1). A test failure follows successful sync hash commit and does not undo it.''')
(HERE/'ontology-changes.json').write_text(json.dumps(changes,indent=2))
print(len(changes),'owning blocks amended')
